e to the module as well as highlighting the target life skill(s) in each session.\n\n# Face Validity  \n\nMost of the participants in both groups were female (19 females VS. 11 males) with ethnicity (14 Malay vs. 7 Chinese and 9 Indian). Although the participants did not live with their parents, 40.2% of them had at least one living parent. Malay Paired Samples t-Test, the results showed that the mean score of 6 coping methods including active coping (t=-5.85, P<0.001), of emotional supports(t =-4.80, P<0.001), of reframing(t=-3.89, P<0.001 ), planning(t=-4.91, P<0.001) and acceptance (t=-5.98, P<0.001) methods significantly increased after interventional sessions. Meanwhile, the mean score of 3 coping methods including selfdenial   (t 2.80, P<0.02) and self-blame (t=7.24, P 0.001) significantly decreased in post-test. No significant change was found in mean score of substance use, behavior disengagement, venting; humor and religion (p>0.05). Using using using\n\n",
